<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ems</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Ems is a river of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, about 370 km long, rising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senne">Senne</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paderborn">Paderborn</a> and flowing north through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-saxony">Lower Saxony</a> to the Dollart estuary and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emden">Emden</a>. In Roman times the Bructeri, Frisii and Chauci lived along it, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-frisia">East Frisia</a> from the Frisian lands to the west. The Dollart forms part of the German–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> border.</p></div>
